Licensing note for the lexsync package
======================================

The MIT licence declared in DESCRIPTION, whose copyright holder and year are in
the LICENSE file, covers the source code of this package only. Three of the data
files bundled under inst/extdata are third-party derivatives carrying their own
terms, which are set out here.


Bundled corpus derivatives
--------------------------

    inst/extdata/en_example.csv
    inst/extdata/es_example.csv
    inst/extdata/zh_example.csv

These small example lexica are derived from the wordfreq package (Speer, 2022,
https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7199437). wordfreq distributes its code and its
data under different terms. The code is under the Apache License 2.0, and the
data files, which are what these three derivatives come from, are
redistributable under

    Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

The three files above are therefore distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0 rather than
under the MIT licence. Anyone redistributing them, or an adaptation of them,
must give appropriate credit to the corpus authors, indicate that changes were
made, and place any adaptation under the same licence. Everything else in the
package, including the other files under inst/extdata, is MIT.


What lexsync changed
--------------------

The frequencies are wordfreq's own Zipf values, carried through unaltered. What
lexsync added is a standardised column layout, shared by every corpus the
package reads, and two orthographic-neighbourhood variables computed here from
the word list itself: Coltheart's N, the count of same-length neighbours
reachable by a single letter substitution, and OLD20, the mean Levenshtein
distance to the twenty nearest words. Each row names its origin in the 'source'
column.


Credit for SUBTLEX
------------------

wordfreq's frequencies blend up to eight domains of text, among them Wikipedia,
OPUS OpenSubtitles, NewsCrawl, Google Books Ngrams, OSCAR, Twitter and Reddit.
The subtitles domain draws in part on the SUBTLEX word lists, which wordfreq
carries by permission on two conditions that pass through to anything derived
from it: work derived from wordfreq must credit the SUBTLEX authors, and it must
remain clear that SUBTLEX is freely available data. Both are honoured here. The
lists are freely available, and their authors are credited as follows.

  Brysbaert, M., & New, B. (2009). SUBTLEX-US. Behavior Research Methods,
  41(4), 977-990. https://doi.org/10.3758/BRM.41.4.977

  van Heuven, W. J. B., Mandera, P., Keuleers, E., & Brysbaert, M. (2014).
  SUBTLEX-UK. Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology, 67(6), 1176-1190.
  https://doi.org/10.1080/17470218.2013.850521

  Cai, Q., & Brysbaert, M. (2010). SUBTLEX-CH. PLoS ONE, 5(6), e10729.
  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0010729

  Brysbaert, M., Buchmeier, M., Conrad, M., Jacobs, A. M., Bölte, J., & Böhl, A.
  (2011). SUBTLEX-DE. Experimental Psychology, 58(5), 412-424.
  https://doi.org/10.1027/1618-3169/a000123

  Keuleers, E., Brysbaert, M., & New, B. (2010). SUBTLEX-NL. Behavior Research
  Methods, 42(3), 643-650. https://doi.org/10.3758/BRM.42.3.643

SUBTLEX contributes to the blend rather than constituting it. No file bundled
here is a copy of a SUBTLEX list, and the SUBTLEX resources the corpus registry
offers are separate corpora a user may fetch directly, not the source of
anything shipped in this package.


Corpora fetched at run time
---------------------------

Corpora downloaded on demand through the registry are not redistributed with
this package. Each carries its own licence and citation, recorded in the
registry entry and reported by list_corpora().


The fuller account
------------------

The repository keeps the complete record, including the provenance caveat that
travels with the Chinese corpus and the retrieval date and checksum of every
derived file:

